  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Since the 2022.1.2 update, I've been experiencing weird physics-based glitches and problems in VRC worlds, particularly with regard to rigidbody collisions.\nI've been working on a racing world and since the update, things are randomly jittering, colliding with nothing or moving at the wrong speeds compared to before. In my racing world, here is how a race is supposed to look and worked in 2022.1.1:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JPs3kzl9nW4\nAnd here is 2022.1.2:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P37Ej4YBSv8\nYou can see just going around the first long turn how your car randomly wobbles around. It also sometimes bumps into stuff around the 2nd to last corner. The best example that's consistently reproducible is that when the race finishes, your car is propelled forwards automatically towards a target transform with your controls disabled. In 2022.1.1, you come to a stop before you reach it but in 2022.1.2 you go too fast and swing back because it gets overshot.\nThe car is a simple rigidbody with box & capsule colliders being propelled forwards by a force, no wheel colliders or other car stuff. I also made sure that none of the colliders have any negative scale values.\nHere is a link to the world (not yet public): https://vrch.at/rrhccsfd\n\n***UPDATE***\nOk, so unrelated to the difference in force physics, the bumping into random stuff looks to be an ObjectSync issue. With that disabled on the player cars, I don't bump into random things or wobble/glitch out at all from what I can tell.\n\n***UPDATE 2***\nIt would appear that these problems only manifest in Desktop Mode. I can't get either of these bugs related to Force or ObjectSync to occur in VR and I'm pretty damn sure all my physics calculations are in FixedUpdate. I noticed that Desktop mode is now locked to 60fps for some reason.",
